<td id="S3.T1.2.6.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S3.T1.2.6.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">85.01</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>VLM zero-shot language-based accuracy on the Cleaner Validation and original Validation sets of ImageNet-1k, sorted by increasing Cleaner Validation accuracy. The standard average prompt ensembling is used. <sup id="S3.T1.7" class="ltx_sup">†</sup>With an OpenCLIP adaptor head.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">VLM zero-shot image classification is based on the similarity (e.g., cosine of the angle) between the embedding of the image and the embeddings of the textual representation of the classes.
The choice of the textual representation
strongly influences VLM recognition accuracy; we study this problem later, in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Text Prompting Techniques for Zero-Shot Classification ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Image Recognition with Vision and Language Embeddings of VLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the basic setup, each class <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in\{1,\ldots,C\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in\{1,\ldots,C\}</annotation></semantics></math> is represented by a text prompt generated by inserting the class name into a natural language template <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>, e.g., <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t(c)=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t(c)=</annotation></semantics></math> ”a photo of a {class name}”.
The functions <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{\text{image}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mtext>image</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{\text{image}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{\text{text}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mtext>text</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{\text{text}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the image and text encoders, respectively, returning unit-normalised embeddings.
Classifier <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> returns the class with maximum cosine similarity to the text prompt:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f_{L}(x,t)=\argmax_{c\in\{1,\cdots,C\}}\cos\left(e_{\text{image}}(x),\ e_{\text{text}}(t(c))\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>cos</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mtext>image</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mtext>text</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{L}(x,t)=\argmax_{c\in\{1,\cdots,C\}}\cos\left(e_{\text{image}}(x),\ e_{\text{text}}(t(c))\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To improve classification performance, prompt ensembling is commonly used. The class embedding is computed as the average over multiple prompts generated by different template functions <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in T</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f^{\mathrm{Avg}}_{L}(x,T)=\argmax_{c\in\{1,\cdots,C\}}\cos\left(e_{\text{image}}(x),\ \frac{1}{|T|}\sum_{t\in T}e_{\text{text}}(t(c))\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>L</mi><mi>Avg</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>cos</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mtext>image</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>e</mi><mtext>text</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\mathrm{Avg}}_{L}(x,T)=\argmax_{c\in\{1,\cdots,C\}}\cos\left(e_{\text{image}}(x),\ \frac{1}{|T|}\sum_{t\in T}e_{\text{text}}(t(c))\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We adopt the standard zero-shot VLM ImageNet-1k evaluation which was introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al.(2021)Radford, Kim, Hallacy, Ramesh, Goh, Agarwal, Sastry, Askell, Mishkin, Clark, Krueger, and Sutskever</a>]</cite>. The average prompt embedding (<a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3.1 Zero-Shot Recognition Baseline ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Image Recognition with Vision and Language Embeddings of VLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) is computed over seven hand-crafted templates.
The prompts do not use ImageNet-1k (WordNet) class names; <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al.(2021)Radford, Kim, Hallacy, Ramesh, Goh, Agarwal, Sastry, Askell, Mishkin, Clark, Krueger, and Sutskever</a>]</cite>
introduced a set of class names that lead to higher recognition accuracy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baseline results</span> of zero-shot language-based accuracy are presented in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.1 Zero-Shot Recognition Baseline ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Image Recognition with Vision and Language Embeddings of VLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
The models are sorted by increasing validation accuracy. The original CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al.(2021)Radford, Kim, Hallacy, Ramesh, Goh, Agarwal, Sastry, Askell, Mishkin, Clark, Krueger, and Sutskever</a>]</cite> is significantly outperformed by all newer models, whose accuracy is in a fairly narrow range of 91-93% on the Cleaner and 83-85% on the original validation, respectively. These results are visualized in the plot shown in Figure <a href="#A1.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Appendix A Additional experiments ‣ Image Recognition with Vision and Language Embeddings of VLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Additional experiments ‣ Image Recognition with Vision and Language Embeddings of VLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Text Prompting Techniques for Zero-Shot Classification</h3>

<figure id="S3.T2" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S3.T2.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S3.T2.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T2.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" rowspan="2"><span id="S3.T2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Template</span></th>
<th id="S3.T2.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" rowspan="2"><span id="S3.T2.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Class Names</span></th>
<td id="S3.T2.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" colspan="3"><span id="S3.T2.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Accuracy (%)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T2.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
