<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>SigLIP 2 (ViT-gopt-16-384) accuracy for different context prompt templates and their combinations with WordNet, OpenAI, OpenAI<sup id="S3.T2.16" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S3.T2.16.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">+</span></sup> class names.
Bottom two rows: results of <math id="S3.T2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-NN using 8 prompts, for details, see text. <span id="S3.T2.17" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;">Green</span>/<span id="S3.T2.18" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#BF0000;">red</span> numbers indicate SigLIP’s <span id="S3.T2.19" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;">outperforming</span>/<span id="S3.T2.20" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#BF0000;">underperforming</span> OpenCLIP.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Many papers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al.(2021)Radford, Kim, Hallacy, Ramesh, Goh, Agarwal, Sastry, Askell, Mishkin, Clark, Krueger, and Sutskever</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al.(2022b)Zhou, Yang, Loy, and Liu</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al.(2022a)Zhou, Yang, Loy, and Liu</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Parisot et al.(2023)Parisot, Yang, and McDonagh</a>]</cite> report that
prompting techniques have a significant impact on zero-shot classification.
This section investigates the topic with the results in Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 3.2 Text Prompting Techniques for Zero-Shot Classification ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Image Recognition with Vision and Language Embeddings of VLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. First, the standard average prompt approach <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al.(2021)Radford, Kim, Hallacy, Ramesh, Goh, Agarwal, Sastry, Askell, Mishkin, Clark, Krueger, and Sutskever</a>]</cite>, referred to as Avg, is evaluated, as well as each of the seven individual templates. Also, a no-context ”{class name}” template is added, which we see as a “natural” prompt. The ensemble of seven standard prompts plus the no-context prompt is denoted Avg<sup id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">′</span></sup>.
We evaluate the influence of the class names with the following sets: WordNet - the original ImageNet-1k class names; OpenAI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al.(2021)Radford, Kim, Hallacy, Ramesh, Goh, Agarwal, Sastry, Askell, Mishkin, Clark, Krueger, and Sutskever</a>]</cite> – the most widely adopted class names for VLM evaluation on ImageNet-1k; and OpenAI<sup id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">+</span></sup> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kisel et al.(2025)Kisel, Volkov, Hanzelková, Janouskova, and Matas</a>]</cite> – an improved version of the OpenAI class names. The latter two aim to improve the alignment between class text representations and the image content and reduce language ambiguity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In addition, Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 3.2 Text Prompting Techniques for Zero-Shot Classification ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Image Recognition with Vision and Language Embeddings of VLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> reports
results using an alternative natural approach of a nearest-neighbour classifier on the 8,000 language embeddings corresponding to all 8 templates applied to 1,000 ImageNet-1k classes.
We report the results of 1-NN (baseline) and 11-NN (highest accuracy).</p>
</div>
