<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 7: </span>Validation accuracy of the few-shot <math id="A1.T7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-NN setup in the SigLIP 2 (ViT-gopt-16-384) image embedding space with a 95% CI.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A1.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.09311v1/figures/images/accuracy_clean_vs_val.png" id="A1.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:357/286;" width="357" height="286" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>VLM zero-shot language-based accuracy on the Cleaner Validation and original Validation sets of ImageNet-1k, with the former on the x-axis and the latter on the y-axis. The results show that no single model consistently outperforms the others across both sets.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A1.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.09311v1/figures/images/k_acc_diff.png" id="A1.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:357/218;" width="357" height="218" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span> Classes showing the largest ImageNet-1k validation accuracy improvements over globally optimal <math id="A1.F5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A1.F5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-NN image classification using SigLip 2 vision embeddings space. Results compare (class-specific optimal <math id="A1.F5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> values, the globally optimal <math id="A1.F5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=9</annotation></semantics></math> value). The y-axis shows classes ranked by improvement magnitude. The results show that the best <math id="A1.F5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-NN settings vary between classes, with accuracy improvements of up to 20%. All of these classes belong to one or more of the following categories: 1. Have high ground truth label noise (e.g. ”catamaran” confused with ”trimarans”). 2. Belong to fine-grained categories (e.g. ”squirrel monkey”, ”partridge”, ”Border collie”, ”Australian terrier”, ”fiddler crab”). 3. Are in the problematic categories identified in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kisel et al.(2025)Kisel, Volkov, Hanzelková, Janouskova, and Matas</a>]</cite> (e.g. ”space bar” is usually present on the image as a part of a ”computer keyboard”, another ImagenNet-1k class). 3. Represent visual concepts that often include many other ImageNet objects in the same image (e.g. ”dock”).</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
